

 Problem Name: CRED Coins
 Objective: Calculate the maximum number of bags Chef can redeem at the CodeChef store using the CRED coins earned from paying bills.
 Redemption Rate: $1 \text{ bag} = 100 \text{ CRED coins}$
 Formula: $\text{Max Bags} = \left\lfloor \frac{X \times Y}{100} \right\rfloor$
 Input Format:
 The first line contains a single integer $T$, denoting the number of test cases.
 Each test case consists of a single line with two space-separated integers: $X$ (coins earned per bill) and $Y$ (number of bills paid).


 Output Format:
 For each test case, output a single integer representing the maximum number of bags Chef can get.


 Constraints:
 $1 \le T \le 100$
 $1 \le X, Y \le 1000$



---

## 2. Initial Implementation (User's Solution)

### Code

```python
t = int(input())
for _ in range(t):
  bills, credX = map(int, input().split())
  credPoints = bills  credX
  bags = credPoints // 100
  print(bags)

```

### Analysis & Review

 Logic & Efficiency: Your solution is optimal! Using integer floor division (`// 100`) directly computes the exact number of complete bags without needing extra imports like `math.floor()`.
 Variable Naming: Descriptive variable names (`bills`, `credX`, `credPoints`, `bags`) make the step-by-step logic very easy to follow. (Note: According to standard input order, the first integer is $X$ [coins per bill] and the second is $Y$ [number of bills], though multiplication is commutative so the output remains identical).
 Formatting Note: Just like the previous snippet, the pasted code contained hidden non-breaking space characters (`\xa0`), which have been cleaned up above to prevent `SyntaxError` issues on online judges.

